    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96371.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "all of you who are wearing blue, because I'm sure you're all secretly Seahawks fans, really. My mom and my biological father were from Wisconsin. Big Green Bay Packers fans football was a big thing in our house. And I knew I was never going to be a football player, but I thought it would be interesting to be a referee. So right out of college, I became a high school football referee in Oregon. And then when I moved up to Seattle area, I continued as a referee up in the Puget Sound area. And, you know, the key things are sometimes I say it's my best training. I say it's sometimes my best training for Congress and was sometimes my best training in the technology industry too. No matter what you decide, someone doesn't like it and you usually get a loud response. But you have to decide. And I think that's so key. You are in a position, you get the best data you can, but it's not perfect data. But you still have to make the decision and you have to, you know, tell people what your decision is right away and it's so important because we're in many places people don't decide and especially in a legislative body folks don't decide and and understanding there are consequences to your decision is also important so that was probably the best training I got for a lot of things I did in the future.",
