    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "a variety of reasons. They couldn't always find the products they wanted because, as you can imagine, if you look at different medical conditions that people might have, there's not always a market where every drug store down the street can carry products, so there are a lot of products that people needed a national market so you could even stock them and have them available. There are embarrassing products that people didn't want to buy and online provided a venue there. But there is also pharmacy and helping people who especially were had with chronic conditions and had drugs, they were getting on a regular basis, be able to get that a place through the mail. And so there was a customer need there, but it's very complicated in terms of the, especially the pharmacy side, in terms of the regulatory environment, insurance, repayments, et cetera. So it was definitely more of a bubble time at that time. People were getting out very quickly. We had great backing from Kynyr Perkins and Mavron in in Seattle area and We had a team of folks my my boss that I worked with at Microsoft then a gentleman named Peter Newpert He and I went together to help start up drugstore and we had a great team of folks who had background in pharmacy and Background in technology and back down in retail and it was that interesting combination of folks too that we needed to bring together Amazon was also an earlier investor, so they helped us kind of figure out the what was going to be necessary for a technology platform, but it was very critical to understand the customer need and how online might change",
