  {
    "episode_id": 96371,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96371.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 71,
    "turn_text": "where, first I'll bring up an old Microsoft example that I think it was very telling. So in my early career at Microsoft, in the early days of Excel, we had to get feedback from customers. We had 1-800 number. So that will really take you back. It was 1-800 Excel wish. I think I can't remember how it adds up to the right number of digits, but it was that idea. And people would call in and give their feedback on what features they wanted to see in Excel. And it turned out that, and I don't remember what the exact number is, but around 70% of the future requests we got in. We're features that were already in the product. But no one knew how to access or use. And I highlight that one, and that one always brings to my mind, because you can think just from a tech standpoint or from an engineering standpoint that, yes, we can do that. But it doesn't really help you out if no one knows how or can figure out how to use it. And it really highlighted how important it is that you interact with your customer and your customer that knows it's there. Because we'd have developers who'd be so frustrated, like,",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
