  {
    "episode_id": 96371,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96371.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "we can do that. Once again, if it's not simple and clear to folks how to use it and it's so important and software the user interface and that interaction and sometimes people forget about that because they're just thinking they're checking off features. And when you're doing a website in particular one for commerce, making sure people can find what they need to find in the easiest way possible, a way that works for them is so important. And that's where just kind of how you categorize things and What's the picture of the product look like? So people know it's actually what they thought it was. Do they know what the name of it is? Or are they more used to the image of it? Or what's the combination? And that's really where we spend a lot of time to help people understand and get the clarity that they needed to make a purchase. And you can put all the information up there, but if you don't put it up in the right way, or people don't recognize the product they're looking for, It doesn't matter if it's in your inventory. And we spend a lot of time iterating on that and getting customer feedback. But to do that, you've got to open up a site and kind of have a beta to get that feedback. So you can't necessarily always do it in a closed room. And that's part of the challenge of e-commerce is opening up enough to interact with folks. But to do that, you also need to have enough depth to be able to have a warehouse and ship. And so you have to do that all together.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
