  {
    "episode_id": 96371,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96371.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 76,
    "turn_text": "One of the interesting things about the commerce space and definitely was true of drugstore in Amazon is there's a huge physical plant side behind it. People talk about the website, but in the end that order has to go through, someone actually has to find it, pick it, put it in a box, ship it out, and take returns. So there's a whole process there that is very involved and, like I said, very capital intensive in terms of putting that together. So understanding how to optimize that is so so important because it can be very expensive if you don't figure out how to optimize it. And I think that's something that, you know, probably Amazon has spent a lot of time doing because they got to the scale where they could help do that. And that's where they do, you know, the work now to figure out how they might get you same day delivery in certain areas because they I think Jeff Bezos says something about being able to predict what you might order so that he can know that he's going to have it in stock in case you need it. That's so critical and yet we've seen other areas. Drugstore was this way for pieces of the business and definitely in the grocery business where you have expiration dates on products and so you end up in a place where if you don't turn products quickly enough then you end up with a lot of bad inventory. And you saw that as in the early days in Seattle area, it was home-grosser, and you saw a lot of businesses like that where they were trying to do a similar type of e-commerce, but realized that, and that last mile, that last delivery mile was pretty expensive. And unless you had enough critical mass, it was hard to do that. So I still think those are things we're working through. That last mile is still critical and can be expensive. and trying to do it same day,",
