    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96371.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 99,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, there's other great start-up areas besides Silicon Valley. From Seattle, folks want to remind everybody about that. And maybe Amy Milman from Springboard out there can talk about this too because she's been working on it for a long, long time. Part of it is making sure that as young people across our country are excited about opportunities and get engaged and involved in learning and love to learn and making sure that we have young women who are engaged and involved in technology and feel like they're opportunities, they're available for them. I was probably more naturally a math and science person, that's where I felt comfortable, so I didn't need the folks to tell me that it's okay to that math is an exciting thing and convinced me. It naturally made sense to me and was something that I wanted to work on. But clearly, if you watch how everyone learns in a different way, we have such an opportunity to make sure kids feel like they have those opportunities. And this because they don't understand something in elementary school right away that they might find a different way to learn than other kids might learn that subject. And they may be something that they're really excited about doing. So to solve these issues, I think we got to start from early learning all the way through in terms of how we describe opportunities to folks. I think the other thing, and you hear this a lot, is that there weren't many women role models. You didn't see many opportunities out there. I was a, when we start up Nimble technology, I was a springboard alum, so springboard was an organization that was helping connect women CEOs with capital that was available because women didn't have as many connections with the venture community. And so even though they had great ideas, they didn't even know who to talk to and didn't have the connections to get funding as much. And so they set up forums just to put women entrepreneurs in front of the venture capital community so they could see some of the great ideas are out there and help facilitate those connections so",
