  {
    "episode_id": 96371,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96371.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 119,
    "turn_text": "you and I'd say that's probably the biggest difference between working in business and talking to customers versus running for office and talking to constituents and to voters. But you have to build that up. You have to build a team of folks. If your team's going to work well together, it's got to be a team that respects each other and is working well together and understands what progress you're making and you're not making. So that's very, very important. And actually, when you get to Congress, it's very entrepreneurial because they just tell you, here's the amount of money you get kind of to spend as a member of Congress. Do what you will. You can hire More people, less people, depending on what you want to do, you can spend money on other things, on technology in your office, for more offices in your district, less, and people make different decisions based on the region they serve in. You've got a member of Congress, one person who serves the whole state of Montana, and then you have someone who might have a few square box in Manhattan, and the needs are very, very different in terms of what they put together. Having that experience of knowing how to put something together from scratch is incredibly helpful for whether it's for a campaign or whether it's actually coming as a member of Congress. I had the opportunity after my first run in 2010, the governor of state of Washington appointed me to run the Department of Revenue for the state. That was a great experience to be an administrator of policy, to work with a team. I brought my team, you know, my experience running a team from things I had done as an entrepreneur, but also at Microsoft. And so, walking in and running a team was not foreign to me. And so, but they were used to political pointees who hadn't done that before, so I was a little bit different there. But seeing the handcuffs that are put on you when you're running an organization, but you have to follow based on particular statutes and laws that were written and how that can be restrictive from trying to get to the end goal you want to have was so important and something that informs me a lot now as a member of Congress. I think it would be great if all members of Congress had to actually administer policy for a while. and see that policy that looks great in theory is not necessarily the most streamlined or efficient way to actually deliver the result you want. And how we look at that and work through that's going to be important if we're going to be good, you know, quick moving",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
