    "turn_text": "bit different because we only have gross receipts taxes based on sales. And right now we have a huge challenge where And we have Amazon in our state, right? So they're charging sales tax for sales within our state. But we have the local running store where people go in and try to shoes and get fitted and then decide to buy online because if they buy online from someone out of state, those folks don't collect sales tax. And in a state like ours, that could be nine and a half percent that they're not paying because they don't pay sales tax, yet All of the service was provided by that local retailer, their paying rent, they hired people, et cetera, and they're in the state working in the community and yet that sale, there's an incentive for some folks to buy from someone out of state because of that price difference. The marketplace fairness act is a piece of legislation that's been worked on for a long time to say, that if you are an out of state retailer, online retailer selling in a state, even if you don't have physical presence there, you're still going to collect that local sales tax to say that there shouldn't be a difference between whether you're a brick and mortar store actually in a state versus an online retailer, you're both going to collect sales tax. Now, if you're a state that doesn't have any sales tax at all. This differentiation is kind of nice because then you can have businesses locate there,",
